As you know, Python is not a low-level programming language like C or C++, 
so it needs an interpreter to convert the code to low-level or machine code 
so that the machine can understand it. that's why we call “Python is an interpreted 
language”. Python uses an interpreter to execute the code line by line.

The Python interpreter is called “CPython”, and it’s written in the C 
programming language. Cpython is the most commonly used Python interpreter, 
but there are many alternatives available such as Jython, PyPy, etc. 
Each has its own properties and characteristics.
When you run the code, the python program interprets each line of code and converts 
it into an intermediate representation called Bytecode. Bytecode is the low-level 
representation of python code. Python does this because the bytecode is easy for 
the interpreter to execute. The bytecode is stored in .pyc files (compiled Python files)
 for future use, so the interpreter doesn’t have to recompile the code each time it is run.

The compiled bytecode (.pyc file) is stored in the same directory as the 
corresponding .py source files by default. The .pyc file has the same base name as 
the .py file but with the .pyc extension. For example, if your Python source 
file is named “new.py”, 
the compiled bytecode file will be “new.pyc”.

Also, python will check if the .pyc file exists or it is up to date every time you run 
the code, If the .pyc file is present and up to date, the interpreter will use it for 
faster execution instead of recompiling the source code to bytecode (.pyc file). otherwise, 
the interpreter will recompile the source code and generate the file new .pyc file.

The bytecode is then executed by the Python Virtual Machine (PVM), 
which is a runtime environment for running Python programs. 
The PVM reads the bytecode and translates it into machine code instructions that can 
be executed by the underlying hardware.

More about PVM
It interprets and executes bytecode generated from Python source code which represents 
actions like assigning variables, calling functions, doing calculations, and controlling 
program flow. It manages memory, automatically cleaning up unused objects using a garbage 
collector (A garbage collector automatically frees up memory occupied by unused objects, 
allowing it to be reused)

Python treats everything as an object (because everything belongs to a class, example — 
An integer variable belongs to integer class), and the PVM implements an object model 
supporting features like inheritance, polymorphism, and encapsulation.

PVM also provides thread safety With a Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) that comes with 
Cpython. which only allows one thread (
A thread is a mini program within a larger program that can run simultaneously with 
other threads) to execute Python bytecode at a time, ensuring thread safety but limited parallelism. 
PVM also includes a complete standard library with modules and functions.

